&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://extensionlaunch.com/blog/browsers-that-support-chrome-extensions?ref=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ExtensionLaunch&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most browsers that support Chrome extensions install yours straight from the Chrome Web Store, so one listing already reaches Edge, Brave, Vivaldi and Opera users. But installing is not the same as running every &lt;code&gt;chrome.*&lt;/code&gt; API. Here is who installs what, which APIs fail outside Google Chrome, and a test pass to run before you write "works in Brave" on your site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Who can install your Chrome Web Store build
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All of these are Chromium-based, except the last two rows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Browser&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Installs from the Chrome Web Store?&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Notes for developers&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Microsoft Edge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Users must allow extensions from other stores the first time. Has its own store, Edge Add-ons&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Brave&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;"Add to Brave". No separate store&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vivaldi&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The Web Store setting must be on. No separate store&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Opera&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Only with an add-on&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Users need Opera's "Install Chrome Extensions" add-on. Has its own store, Opera add-ons&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Arc&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In maintenance mode since May 2025&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Comet, Dia&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reported to work&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Very little vendor documentation, so test before you promise support&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Firefox&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Needs its own build, signed by Mozilla, on AMO&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Safari&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Needs a Safari web extension inside an app, shipped through the App Store&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a sense of scale, the original guide cites StatCounter's desktop share for September 2026: Chrome 65.09%, Edge 14.64%, Safari 8.06%, Firefox 6.11% and Opera 2.38%. Brave and Vivaldi identify as Chrome, so their users are counted inside Chrome's number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The APIs that quietly fail
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Microsoft's supported APIs page lists these as &lt;strong&gt;not available in Edge&lt;/strong&gt; (leaving aside ChromeOS-only APIs):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;identity.getAuthToken&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;identity.getAccounts&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;gcm&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;instanceID&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;readingList&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;devtools.recorder&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google sign-in causes the most trouble. &lt;code&gt;getAuthToken&lt;/code&gt; returns a token for the Google account signed in to Chrome. Edge doesn't support it, and a developer on Brave's forum reported it failing in Brave 1.70 with "Custom URI scheme is not supported on Chrome apps", even though the same code ran fine in Chrome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is to try the native call first, then fall back to a standard OAuth web flow through &lt;code&gt;launchWebAuthFlow&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// WEB_CLIENT_ID: an OAuth client of type "Web application"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;webFlowToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;authUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;URLSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;client_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;WEB_CLIENT_ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;response_type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'token'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;redirect_uri&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;identity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRedirectURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...chromiumapp.org&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;scope&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'openid email profile'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;identity&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;launchWebAuthFlow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;interactive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;redirect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;URLSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;redirect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'access_token'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;googleToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;identity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getAuthToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;interactive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;webFlowToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Edge, Brave and other non-Google Chromium builds&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two things to remember: add the &lt;code&gt;getRedirectURL()&lt;/code&gt; value as an authorized redirect URI on that OAuth client, and expect to ask the user again when the token expires, because the web-flow token doesn't refresh itself the way &lt;code&gt;getAuthToken&lt;/code&gt; does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few other Google-only pieces need the same care:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Chrome Web Store payments&lt;/strong&gt; were retired long ago, and Edge never supported extensions built on them. Use your own billing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Chrome's built-in AI APIs&lt;/strong&gt; depend on models that ship with Google Chrome. Feature-detect them and keep a cloud fallback.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;UI placement varies.&lt;/strong&gt; Edge shows &lt;code&gt;sidePanel&lt;/code&gt; extensions in its own sidebar, so check your screenshots and onboarding copy in each browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The general rule: check that an API exists before you call it, catch errors from calls that exist but fail, and degrade gracefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A test pass you can run today
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need a separate build. Every Chromium browser has Developer mode and &lt;strong&gt;Load unpacked&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build exactly what you upload to the Chrome Web Store.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Load it at &lt;code&gt;edge://extensions&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;brave://extensions&lt;/code&gt;, or Vivaldi's Tools &amp;gt; Extensions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Walk through install, onboarding, sign-in, the main feature, and an update.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Watch the service worker console for errors you never see in Chrome, especially around identity, side panels and new tab overrides.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Watch for quirks that are specific to one browser. &lt;strong&gt;Brave Shields&lt;/strong&gt; blocks many third-party scripts and trackers by default, so features that read ad or analytics elements may behave differently. In &lt;strong&gt;Vivaldi&lt;/strong&gt;, a new tab override only appears after the user enables "Controlled by Extension" under Settings &amp;gt; Tabs &amp;gt; New Tab Page. Put that in your onboarding, or users will assume your extension is broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To tag support tickets or analytics by browser, read the user-agent brands:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;brands&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;navigator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;userAgentData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;brands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;brand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;known&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Microsoft Edge'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Brave'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Opera'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;brands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;browser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;known&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;brands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Google Chrome'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Chrome'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Other Chromium'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Not every Chromium browser adds its own brand, so treat "Other Chromium" as unknown rather than as Chrome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When a second listing is worth it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your Chrome Web Store listing already covers Brave and Vivaldi, which have no stores of their own. Add another listing when:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Edge users are a real share of your audience.&lt;/strong&gt; An Edge Add-ons listing removes the "allow other stores" step and reaches people who only search Microsoft's store. It takes the same ZIP.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;You want Firefox or Safari users.&lt;/strong&gt; There is no shortcut there.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A store has discovery features you want&lt;/strong&gt;, such as Opera's Recommended section or Firefox's Recommended Extensions program.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then name the browsers you test on your site, next to the install button. Many users never ask whether a "Chrome" extension works for them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Key takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Edge, Brave and Vivaldi install from the Chrome Web Store; Opera needs an add-on first; Firefox and Safari need their own builds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;getAuthToken&lt;/code&gt; fails in Edge and has been reported failing in Brave. Fall back to &lt;code&gt;launchWebAuthFlow&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Feature-detect Google-only APIs, including the built-in AI APIs, and degrade gracefully.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test with Load unpacked in each browser your users report, and check the service worker console.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;List the browsers you support where users decide to install.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full &lt;a href="https://extensionlaunch.com/blog/browsers-that-support-chrome-extensions?ref=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;developer guide to browsers that support Chrome extensions&lt;/a&gt; has the vendor sources, an FAQ and the Opera and Arc details. When you're ready to ship to all of them, you can &lt;a href="https://extensionlaunch.com/?ref=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;launch your browser extension on ExtensionLaunch&lt;/a&gt; to get a launch page you can share in any browser's community.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which non-Chrome browser has caused you the most surprising extension bug?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It was during the final ten minutes of a Jumu'ah prayer. The mosque was silent, save for the soft cadence of the imam. Suddenly, a sharp, upbeat ringtone cut through the stillness like a knife. Every head turned. The person responsible didn't just look embarrassed; they looked devastated. I sat there, phone in my pocket, and realized I had been that person exactly three weeks prior during a high-stakes job interview. We all have these moments where our devices fail to respect the environment, and we simply forget to manage our own volume.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fundamental issue is that humans are terrible at context-switching. We are expected to remember to silence our phones before entering a meeting, then remember to turn them back up afterward. If we miss one step, we face social friction or missed calls. Existing solutions usually suffer from a massive flaw: they are either too manual, requiring the user to tap a button every single time, or they are battery-hungry monsters that poll the GPS location every few seconds. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to build something that felt like it wasn't even there. I needed an automation tool that would trigger based on location, but I couldn't afford to burn through a user's battery life in two hours. Most developers try to solve this by manually requesting location updates via the &lt;code&gt;LocationManager&lt;/code&gt;, which is a recipe for disaster. If you aren't careful with &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;setFastestInterval&lt;/code&gt;, your app will wake up the processor constantly, causing the dreaded 'Android System' battery drain that users hate. I needed a way to define a geofence and let the OS handle the heavy lifting while my app stayed asleep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The technical decision / implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I decided to bypass the &lt;code&gt;LocationManager&lt;/code&gt; entirely and rely on the &lt;code&gt;GeofencingClient&lt;/code&gt; within the Google Play Services library. This was a deliberate choice to shift the responsibility of location monitoring from my process to the system-level location service. By using the &lt;code&gt;addGeofences()&lt;/code&gt; method, I am essentially handing a set of coordinates and a radius to the Android OS. The system then monitors these boundaries using a combination of cell tower triangulation, Wi-Fi scanning, and GPS, choosing the most energy-efficient method depending on the current state of the device.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the device crosses the defined boundary, the OS fires a &lt;code&gt;PendingIntent&lt;/code&gt;. This is the core of the architecture: my app does not need to be 'running' in the foreground to receive this event. I registered a &lt;code&gt;BroadcastReceiver&lt;/code&gt; that acts as the entry point for these triggers. When the receiver catches the transition, it triggers a &lt;code&gt;JobIntentService&lt;/code&gt; or a &lt;code&gt;WorkManager&lt;/code&gt; task to execute the volume change. This ensures that the code runs reliably even if the app is killed by the system to reclaim memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;kotlin&lt;br&gt;
val geofence = Geofence.Builder()&lt;br&gt;
    .setRequestId("office_zone")&lt;br&gt;
    .setCircularRegion(lat, lon, 100f)&lt;br&gt;
    .setExpirationDuration(Geofence.NEVER_EXPIRE)&lt;br&gt;
    .setTransitionTypes(Geofence.GEOFENCE_TRANSITION_ENTER or Geofence.GEOFENCE_TRANSITION_EXIT)&lt;br&gt;
    .build()&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;geofencingClient.addGeofences(geofencingRequest, geofencePendingIntent)&lt;br&gt;
    .addOnSuccessListener { /* Successfully registered */ }&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach is opinionated: I prioritize battery life over millisecond precision. Because the OS batches location events to save power, there might be a delay of thirty seconds to a minute before the 'Enter' event triggers. For an app like Muffle, where the goal is to silence a phone when you arrive at a destination, that minor delay is a completely acceptable trade-off compared to the alternative of a phone that dies by noon.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What surprised you / what you'd do differently
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most humbling lessons I learned was the instability of the GPS signal in 'indoor' environments. I assumed that if I set a geofence around a building, the phone would trigger the moment I walked through the door. In practice, modern skyscrapers and thick-walled older buildings wreak havoc on GPS accuracy. I spent weeks debugging why my app wasn't triggering for users in certain office buildings, only to realize that the GPS signal was bouncing off walls, causing the 'current location' to jump hundreds of meters away from the actual coordinates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I initially tried to fix this by decreasing the radius, thinking smaller was better. This was a mistake. A smaller radius makes the geofence more sensitive to those random GPS 'jumps', causing the volume to flip-flop between silent and normal while the user is sitting still. I learned that you have to use a buffer—a larger radius that accounts for the fact that a phone's location estimate is a probability cloud, not a pinprick. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I were to rebuild this today, I would implement a 'confidence filter' in the logic. Instead of reacting to the very first geofence event, I would wait for a secondary signal or a confirmation from the network location provider before actually changing the system volume. I also underestimated how much device-specific power management would interfere. Some manufacturers, like Xiaomi or Samsung, have aggressive battery saving features that kill background &lt;code&gt;BroadcastReceiver&lt;/code&gt; components even when they are registered correctly. I had to include specific instructions for users to exclude the app from battery optimization, which feels like a failure of the platform's consistency rather than my own code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Practical takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The biggest takeaway from this journey is that battery efficiency is not about how you write your code, but about which APIs you choose to delegate work to. If you are building an app that requires location awareness, stop trying to write your own location-tracking loop. Trust the system-provided APIs, accept their limitations, and design your user experience around the reality of these constraints. Don't hide the latency; instead, design the UI to acknowledge that a state change might take a few moments to sync.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automation should solve human friction, not create technical debt. If you are curious about how this looks in a production environment, you can look at the implementation of Muffle: &lt;a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.muffle.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.muffle.app&lt;/a&gt;. It is a lesson in managing local data without needing a cloud backend, keeping everything on-device to ensure that privacy remains intact while the automation runs quietly in the background. Whether you are building an automation tool or a data-heavy utility, always ask yourself if you can let the OS handle the 'when' while you focus strictly on the 'what'.&lt;/p&gt;

