&lt;h2&gt;


  What a detection proves, and what it leaves open
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A count of how many websites use jQuery is only as good as its definition of a detection. Here a detection means the page webatla's scanner fetched carried the jQuery script. The 325.8 million base includes parked domains and placeholder pages, and the 11.54% jQuery market share is measured against all of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Versions come from a subset. Only about 14.0 million jQuery sites, 37.3% of the total, expose a version string the scan can read, and every version share in this post is a share of that subset. A copy compiled into a larger bundle can escape both the scanner and the curl check below, though the console check still finds it when the bundle exposes the global &lt;code&gt;jQuery&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How to check a site's jQuery version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You do not need a crawler to see what a given site ships. Two quick looks cover most cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From a terminal, fetch the HTML and print every script tag that mentions jquery:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -sL https://example.com/ &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -ioE &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'&amp;lt;script[^&amp;gt;]*jquery[^&amp;gt;]*&amp;gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; sort -u
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Things to look for in the output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A path under &lt;code&gt;/wp-includes/js/jquery/&lt;/code&gt; is the copy that ships with WordPress core. WordPress adds a &lt;code&gt;?ver=&lt;/code&gt; query string to the URL, and for the bundled jQuery that value is normally the library version. The same folder holds &lt;code&gt;jquery-migrate.min.js&lt;/code&gt;, whose &lt;code&gt;?ver=&lt;/code&gt; value names the Migrate release.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A host of &lt;code&gt;code.jquery.com&lt;/code&gt; means the page loads the file from the official jQuery CDN. Check whether the same tag has an &lt;code&gt;integrity&lt;/code&gt; attribute. Without one, the browser runs whatever that host returns.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A file name like &lt;code&gt;jquery-3.7.1.min.js&lt;/code&gt; often carries the version, but nothing forces a file name to be accurate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This only catches tags that sit in the initial HTML on a single line. Scripts injected later by a loader, or merged into one big bundle, will not appear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the browser, open DevTools on the page and paste this into the console.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;core&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jquery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;migrate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;migrateVersion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;migrateWarnings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;migrateWarnings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;jQuery.fn.jquery&lt;/code&gt; holds the version string of the copy bound to the global &lt;code&gt;jQuery&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;migrateVersion&lt;/code&gt; is set by jQuery Migrate 3.x, and &lt;code&gt;migrateWarnings&lt;/code&gt; collects the deprecated calls the page has made so far, even in the minified build that keeps the console quiet. If all three come back &lt;code&gt;undefined&lt;/code&gt;, jQuery is most likely absent, or it lives inside a module bundle that never touches &lt;code&gt;window&lt;/code&gt;. When a page loads two copies, the global &lt;code&gt;jQuery&lt;/code&gt; points to whichever ran last (unless the code calls &lt;code&gt;jQuery.noConflict(true)&lt;/code&gt;), and plugins registered on the earlier copy are not visible on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  jQuery versions below the 3.0, 3.4 and 3.5 security fixes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three releases in jQuery's history matter to security tools, because each one closed a published advisory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.0.0&lt;/strong&gt; fixed CVE-2015-9251. A cross-domain Ajax request made without a &lt;code&gt;dataType&lt;/code&gt; option could execute a script response.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.4.0&lt;/strong&gt; fixed CVE-2019-11358, a prototype pollution bug in deep &lt;code&gt;jQuery.extend(true, ...)&lt;/code&gt; merges.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.5.0&lt;/strong&gt; fixed CVE-2020-11022 and CVE-2020-11023. HTML from an untrusted source passed to methods such as &lt;code&gt;.html()&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;.append()&lt;/code&gt; could run code, even after sanitizing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sorting the 25 most common releases on webatla's jQuery page by those three lines gives the following split.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6vml5bsmmdp6mfymugva.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6vml5bsmmdp6mfymugva.png" alt="Lollipop chart of jQuery sites grouped by release line: 3.5.0 and newer 4.49 million (32.0%), 3.4.x 501 thousand (3.6%), 3.1.x to 3.3.x 2.10 million (14.9%), 2.x 781 thousand (5.6%), 1.x 4.23 million (30.1%) of sites that expose a version" width="799" height="467"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Of the roughly 14.0 million jQuery sites that expose a version, at least 7.6 million run a release older than 3.5.0. Groups cover the 25 most common releases.



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;At least 7.6 million (54.2%) run something older than 3.5.0.&lt;/strong&gt; That combines the 3.4.x, 3.1.x to 3.3.x, 2.x and 1.x groups.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;At least 7.1 million (50.6%) predate 3.4.0.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;At least 5.0 million (35.7%) predate 3.0.0&lt;/strong&gt;, which means a 1.x or 2.x build.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;At least 4.49 million (32.0%) run 3.5.0 or newer.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"At least" applies because the remaining 1,111 version strings, about 1.9 million sites, are left out of the grouping, and part of them belongs to the old lines too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The single most common release is 3.5.1, on 1,400,181 sites, so the busiest version sits just past the last line. The last release before the 3.5.0 line, 3.4.1, still runs on 501,196 sites. That step is also the classic upgrade trap. Release 3.5.0 changed how &lt;code&gt;jQuery.htmlPrefilter&lt;/code&gt; treats self-closing tag shorthand in HTML strings, so markup built that way can render differently after the bump. Test the templates that build HTML in JavaScript before you ship it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The groups translate into a rough triage for a site you look after.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.5.0 or newer.&lt;/strong&gt; Keep it on the latest 3.x release. jQuery 4.0 is out, and jquery.com itself shows 4.0.0 in the sample rows of webatla's page, but no 4.x string makes the 25 most common, so each one sits below the 138,361 sites of 1.7.1. Treat a move to 4.x as its own major upgrade, since it removes further deprecated APIs such as &lt;code&gt;jQuery.trim&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.0 to 3.4.&lt;/strong&gt; Upgrade within the 3.x line, then retest every place that builds HTML strings or deep-merges objects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;1.9 to 2.x.&lt;/strong&gt; Load jQuery Migrate 3.x next to a current 3.x core in a staging copy, work through the warnings it records, then remove the shim. Skipping that step tends to break on APIs that 3.0 removed outright, such as &lt;code&gt;.size()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Older than 1.9.&lt;/strong&gt; Go through Migrate 1.x on a 1.9 or later core first. Among the 25 most common releases, 1.8.3, 1.7.2, 1.7.1 and 1.4.2 alone account for 1,106,963 sites.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of this proves a site can be attacked. The 3.5.0 advisories matter when untrusted HTML reaches those methods, and the 3.4.0 one when untrusted objects get deep-merged. Version-based tools judge by the string, though. &lt;code&gt;npm audit&lt;/code&gt; flags an old jQuery installed as a package, and scanners that read the library version from a served page, such as Retire.js, flag a pre-3.5.0 file whatever the code does with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;jQuery UI has its own line at 1.13.0, which fixed several cross-site scripting issues in widget options such as those of the datepicker. On webatla's jQuery UI page, at least 534,782 of roughly 773,000 sites with a known version (69.1%) run an older release, and at least 114,000 (14.8%) run 1.13.0 or newer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  jQuery Migrate on 16.5 million sites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://webatla.com/technology/jquery-migrate" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;jQuery Migrate&lt;/a&gt; restores APIs that newer jQuery releases removed or changed, and records a warning each time old code calls one. It was designed as a bridge for the duration of an upgrade. webatla detects it on 16,519,723 sites, a number equal to 43.9% of the jQuery count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhk46t76chrtgcly8r105.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhk46t76chrtgcly8r105.png" alt="Ranked list of jQuery Migrate version strings: 3.4.1 on 12,858,135 sites (87.3%), 1.4.1 on 655,960, 3.3.2 on 550,739, 1.2.1 on 248,422, 3.4.0 on 143,206, 3.0.1 on 101,751, 3.0.0 on 31,892" width="800" height="514"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Where a jQuery Migrate version is visible, 87.3% of sites run 3.4.1, which alone accounts for 12,858,135 sites.



&lt;p&gt;The likely source of that single release is WordPress. Core registers &lt;code&gt;jquery-migrate&lt;/code&gt; as a dependency of its &lt;code&gt;jquery&lt;/code&gt; script handle, so any theme or plugin that enqueues jQuery gets the shim too. The counts cannot prove it, but the &lt;code&gt;?ver=&lt;/code&gt; value on the &lt;code&gt;jquery-migrate&lt;/code&gt; tag in the curl output above shows which Migrate release a WordPress site serves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The older Migrate line is still around too. Migrate 1.x was the bridge for the move to jQuery 1.9, and 1.x strings appear on at least 931,810 sites, led by 1.4.1 with 655,960. A 1.x shim is a fair hint that the core under it is old as well. A few values in the version column, such as 9, 83 and 2026, are not Migrate release numbers at all, so read that column as what the page declared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The more telling number is how Migrate moved relative to jQuery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkrnjytv28llglwtx270i.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkrnjytv28llglwtx270i.png" alt="Line chart of jQuery Migrate sites as a percent of jQuery sites from 2015 to October 2026: about 30% in 2015, 31.5% at the end of 2020, 38.4% at the end of 2023 and 43.8% in October 2026" width="800" height="457"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
In the history model, jQuery Migrate sites went from 31.5% of the jQuery count at the end of 2020 to 43.8% in October 2026.



&lt;p&gt;From October 2023 to October 2026 jQuery changed by -20%, while Migrate changed by -7.8%. The model's year-end totals show the same thing from another side. jQuery went from 47.1 million at the end of 2023 to 37.9 million in October 2026, and Migrate from 18.1 million to 16.6 million. Since Migrate cannot run without jQuery, most of jQuery's net loss most likely came from sites that did not load the shim. The jQuery sites that remain carry it more and more often. The early years of the series are approximate, which does not change the direction since 2020.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a maintainer, Migrate on a page is weak evidence on its own. The page may still depend on behavior that current jQuery dropped, or it may simply run on WordPress defaults. What the shim does give you is a to-do list, as long as you read it in the right place. The unminified &lt;code&gt;jquery-migrate.js&lt;/code&gt; prints each warning to the console with a &lt;code&gt;JQMIGRATE&lt;/code&gt; prefix. The minified build, the one WordPress serves by default, is muted. It logs one startup line saying Migrate is installed, which is not a warning, and stays quiet after that. Both builds fill &lt;code&gt;jQuery.migrateWarnings&lt;/code&gt;. Load the page, click through the main flows, then read that array. On a staging copy you can also load the unminified file, or set &lt;code&gt;jQuery.migrateMute = false&lt;/code&gt; before you click through, to see each warning as it happens. Remove the shim when the array stays empty. The reverse check does not work. A page without Migrate may simply have dropped the shim and kept the old calls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What else on the page needs jQuery
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Taking jQuery out is rarely about your own &lt;code&gt;$()&lt;/code&gt; calls. The harder part is everything written as a jQuery plugin.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bootstrap 3 and 4.&lt;/strong&gt; Their JavaScript components, such as dropdowns, modals and collapse, are jQuery plugins, so a page that uses them needs jQuery loaded first. Bootstrap 5 removed that dependency. webatla detects Bootstrap on 13,848,301 sites, and about 5.39 million of them show a version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj4j1686s0gyfr0edkses.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj4j1686s0gyfr0edkses.png" alt="Three numbers: 1,159,554 Bootstrap 3.x sites and 831,649 Bootstrap 4.x sites, whose JavaScript plugins require jQuery, and 2,696,639 Bootstrap 5.x sites with no jQuery dependency" width="800" height="339"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Among Bootstrap sites on the 25 most common version strings, 1,159,554 run 3.x and 831,649 run 4.x, two lines whose JavaScript plugins need jQuery.



&lt;p&gt;Added up across the 25 most common Bootstrap version strings, 1,159,554 sites run 3.x and 831,649 run 4.x, together about 37% of Bootstrap sites with a known version. Another 2,696,639 run 5.x. A Bootstrap 3 or 4 site that uses only the stylesheet has no jQuery need from Bootstrap, so treat those two numbers as a ceiling for this dependency within the sample, not a count of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;jQuery UI and jQuery Mobile.&lt;/strong&gt; jQuery UI runs on 1,853,686 sites and jQuery Mobile on 372,751. Both are widget layers built on top of jQuery, so neither survives its removal. They are also shrinking faster than jQuery itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fulcxog8fxld635yrnqp3.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fulcxog8fxld635yrnqp3.png" alt="Diverging lollipop chart of the three-year change in sites: jQuery-pjax +3.8%, jQuery Migrate -7.8%, jQuery Modal -12%, jQuery CDN -17%, jQuery -20%, jQuery Mobile -25%, jQuery UI -32%" width="800" height="449"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
From October 2023 to October 2026, jQuery UI changed by -32% and jQuery Mobile by -25%, against -20% for jQuery itself.



&lt;p&gt;The jQuery CDN entry, which marks pages that load jQuery from a public delivery network, sits at 2,320,916 sites today and changed by -17% over those three years. Migrate, at -7.8%, is the slowest decliner in the chart. jQuery-pjax, a small plugin on 92,159 sites, is the only one up over the three years, by 3.8%, though its last 12 months show -3.1%.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A workable order for a removal follows from this. List the plugins that hang off &lt;code&gt;jQuery.fn&lt;/code&gt;, replace or retire the widgets built on jQuery UI or Bootstrap 3 and 4, clear the Migrate warnings, and delete the core file last. Doing it in the opposite order is how a page ends up with a dead datepicker in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How many sites remove jQuery each year
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The jQuery usage statistics on the technology page put the change at -9.1% over the last 12 months and -20% from October 2023 to October 2026. The history model also counts sites that dropped jQuery and stayed online, a rough yearly measure of removal work. That figure rose each year from 2022 to 2025, at 5.27 million, 5.47 million, 6.6 million and 7.24 million. January to October 2026 shows 4.75 million, below the roughly 6.4 million of the same ten months in 2025. These are model figures, so read them as a trend rather than exact counts. In those ten months of 2026, 5.1 million new sites also launched with jQuery already on the page. At the 12-month pace the count would stay in the tens of millions for years, which makes jQuery knowledge mostly maintenance knowledge: reading an old plugin, upgrading across the 3.5.0 change, clearing Migrate warnings and swapping widgets for native browser APIs or a component library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Questions developers ask about these counts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Does a jQuery detection mean the site's own code uses jQuery?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. It means the page the scanner fetched carried the jQuery script. A theme, a plugin or a single widget may be the only thing that calls it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How do I find the jQuery version of a site?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;jQuery.fn.jquery&lt;/code&gt; in the DevTools console, or look at the script URL in the HTML for a versioned file name or a &lt;code&gt;?ver=&lt;/code&gt; parameter. In webatla's data, about 37.3% of jQuery sites show a version string at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is a site on a release older than 3.5.0 vulnerable?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It runs library code with known advisories. Whether that can be exploited depends on how the site's own code uses &lt;code&gt;.html()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.append()&lt;/code&gt; or deep &lt;code&gt;jQuery.extend&lt;/code&gt; merges. Version-based audits will flag it either way, and moving to 3.5.0 or later clears the advisories listed above from the report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Why does jQuery Migrate show up on so many sites?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most likely because WordPress core loads it next to jQuery by default. 16,519,723 sites load the shim, and a single default would explain why 87.3% of those with a visible version run the same release, 3.4.1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://webatla.com/blog/jquery-statistics" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;jQuery Statistics 2026: How Many Websites Use jQuery?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://webatla.com/blog/websites-created-using-wordpress" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WordPress Statistics 2026: How Many Websites Use WordPress?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Webhook handlers often reject valid payloads with an HTTP &lt;code&gt;400&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;401&lt;/code&gt; during local development. A trailing newline in an environment variable, a JSON body parsed before verification, or a signing secret copied from the wrong place is enough to break a signature check. Triggering live events from a provider dashboard over and over is a slow way to find out which one it was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A tunnel with a request inspector gives you the method, path, headers, status and (if you keep it) body of each delivery. This guide covers why signature checks fail, what each provider sends, how to check signatures with the InstaWormhole Request Inspector, and how to replay a captured request after you fix your handler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why local webhook signature verification fails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stripe, GitHub, Shopify and Slack all sign deliveries with HMAC-SHA256 and a shared secret. Your server recomputes the digest from the incoming request and compares it with the value in the signature header. If the two differ, the request must be rejected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four causes account for most local failures:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Body parser mutation:&lt;/strong&gt; Frameworks such as Express often parse JSON before your webhook route runs. Re-serializing the parsed object does not reliably reproduce the exact bytes the provider signed, so the digest no longer matches. Shopify’s documentation lists parsing the body before verification as a common issue and tells you to place verification middleware before any body parser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Wrong signing secret:&lt;/strong&gt; A secret copied from a different endpoint, app or environment will never match. Shopify, for example, signs with your app’s client secret rather than a separate webhook secret. GitHub does not send &lt;code&gt;X-Hub-Signature-256&lt;/code&gt; at all if no secret is configured on the webhook.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Header and prefix handling:&lt;/strong&gt; Header names are case-insensitive, and Slack’s documentation warns against assuming a particular letter case. Signature values also carry prefixes you must handle: &lt;code&gt;sha256=&lt;/code&gt; for GitHub, &lt;code&gt;v0=&lt;/code&gt; for Slack, and a &lt;code&gt;t=...,v1=...&lt;/code&gt; structure for Stripe.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Timestamp tolerance:&lt;/strong&gt; Stripe and Slack include a timestamp in the signed material. Stripe’s libraries reject timestamps outside a five-minute default tolerance (configurable), and Slack’s documentation recommends rejecting requests more than five minutes from local time. A drifting clock or a late replay fails the check even when the secret is correct.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Compute the HMAC over the raw request body bytes before any JSON middleware touches them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Provider signature headers and algorithms
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Provider&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Signature header&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Timestamp in signed material&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Format&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stripe&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Stripe-Signature&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, &lt;code&gt;t=&lt;/code&gt; inside the same header&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;t=&amp;lt;unix seconds&amp;gt;,v1=&amp;lt;hex&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, HMAC-SHA256 over &lt;code&gt;{t}.{raw body}&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;X-Hub-Signature-256&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;sha256=&amp;lt;hex&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shopify&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;X-Shopify-Hmac-Sha256&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Base64 HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Slack&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;X-Slack-Signature&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, &lt;code&gt;X-Slack-Request-Timestamp&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;v0=&amp;lt;hex&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, HMAC-SHA256 over &lt;code&gt;v0:{timestamp}:{raw body}&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Stripe
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stripe signs the timestamp from the header, a literal dot, and the raw payload with your endpoint’s signing secret, and sends the result as the &lt;code&gt;v1&lt;/code&gt; value. During a secret rotation the header can carry more than one &lt;code&gt;v1&lt;/code&gt; entry, so compare against each. Stripe’s own libraries handle all of this for you; see &lt;a href="https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Stripe’s webhook documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  GitHub
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GitHub computes an HMAC hex digest of the payload with your webhook’s secret token, and the value always starts with &lt;code&gt;sha256=&lt;/code&gt;. The older &lt;code&gt;X-Hub-Signature&lt;/code&gt; header uses HMAC-SHA1 and is included only for legacy purposes, so use the &lt;code&gt;-256&lt;/code&gt; header. GitHub’s guidance is to compare with a constant-time function such as &lt;code&gt;crypto.timingSafeEqual&lt;/code&gt;. See &lt;a href="https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/validating-webhook-deliveries" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Validating webhook deliveries&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Shopify
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shopify sends a Base64-encoded HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body, keyed with your app’s client secret. A hex digest will never equal a Base64 header, so encode your digest as Base64 before comparing. Shopify also sends an &lt;code&gt;X-Shopify-Webhook-Id&lt;/code&gt; header that is unique per delivery, which you can use to detect duplicates. It expects a &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt;-range response, treats redirects as errors, and applies a one-second connection timeout and a five-second timeout for the whole request. See &lt;a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/webhooks/verify-deliveries" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Shopify’s delivery verification guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Slack
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Slack joins the literal &lt;code&gt;v0&lt;/code&gt;, the &lt;code&gt;X-Slack-Request-Timestamp&lt;/code&gt; value and the raw body with colons, signs that string with your app’s signing secret, and sends &lt;code&gt;v0=&amp;lt;hex digest&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. Because the timestamp is inside the signed string, a captured request cannot be replayed indefinitely against a strict verifier. See &lt;a href="https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/verifying-requests-from-slack" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Verifying requests from Slack&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to look for in the inspector
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each captured request, check the following.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Method and path:&lt;/strong&gt; Confirm the request arrived as a &lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt; and that the path matches the route your app mounts, including any prefix such as &lt;code&gt;/api/webhooks/stripe&lt;/code&gt;. A redirect response is a failure for Shopify, so avoid trailing-slash redirects on webhook routes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Signature header:&lt;/strong&gt; Confirm &lt;code&gt;Stripe-Signature&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;X-Hub-Signature-256&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;X-Shopify-Hmac-Sha256&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;X-Slack-Signature&lt;/code&gt; actually arrived. A missing header usually points to a missing secret on the provider side or a proxy that dropped it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Content type:&lt;/strong&gt; Slack slash commands and interactive payloads are form-encoded, while Events API deliveries and most other providers send JSON. Either way, sign-check the raw body, not a parsed copy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Status and who answered:&lt;/strong&gt; The inspector shows the status and whether your app or InstaWormhole itself produced the answer. Your own handler decides which code it returns, so choose them deliberately. In the example below, &lt;code&gt;400&lt;/code&gt; means a malformed or stale request and &lt;code&gt;401&lt;/code&gt; means a signature mismatch. A &lt;code&gt;500&lt;/code&gt; often means an exception in your verification code, such as passing an &lt;code&gt;undefined&lt;/code&gt; secret to &lt;code&gt;crypto.createHmac()&lt;/code&gt; because an environment variable did not load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If InstaWormhole answered instead of your app, the request never reached your handler. Tunnel Doctor reports a code for each layer. &lt;code&gt;CONNECTOR_OFFLINE&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;CONNECTOR_UNAVAILABLE&lt;/code&gt; mean the connector is not running or did not take the request, and &lt;code&gt;ORIGIN_UNREACHABLE&lt;/code&gt; means your app is not answering on the &lt;code&gt;--origin&lt;/code&gt; address.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step by step: tunnel, inspect, verify
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The inspector records requests only for durable tunnels, so create one in the dashboard rather than using a quick address.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 1: Connect your app
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a quick, temporary address, the connector’s quick form takes a port:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx @instawormhole/connector 3000
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That form is not recorded in the inspector. For webhook debugging, create a durable tunnel in the dashboard and copy the command from its Connect an app section. It has this shape:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx @instawormhole/connector run --relay wss://relay.instawormhole.site --origin 127.0.0.1:3000 --stored-device --tunnel &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;tunnel id&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add your webhook path to the tunnel’s public address and enter that URL in the provider’s dashboard, for example &lt;code&gt;https://example.instawormhole.site/webhooks/stripe&lt;/code&gt;. Use a test or sandbox mode: the InstaWormhole docs advise against pointing a provider’s live mode at a tunnel on your laptop. Stripe’s own &lt;code&gt;listen&lt;/code&gt; command also forwards events to a local address and works as an alternative for Stripe-only testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 2: Keep request bodies
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By default the inspector does not store bodies. Open the inspector and choose Keep bodies before you send the test event. New requests then keep the first 64 KiB of the request and response body for 24 hours. Bodies are not redacted, so turn capture off when you are done and delete requests you no longer need. The inspector does redact sensitive header values such as authorization and cookie, and query string values in the path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 3: Verify the raw body in your handler
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This Express example reads raw bytes on webhook routes and uses constant-time comparison. For production Stripe handling, prefer the official SDK’s &lt;code&gt;stripe.webhooks.constructEvent()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"node:crypto"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"express"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Raw bytes on webhook routes only; no JSON parser before these handlers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;use&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/webhooks"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"*/*"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;timingSafeMatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;actual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Buffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"utf8"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Buffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;actual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"utf8"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;timingSafeEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;TOLERANCE_SECONDS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isFresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timestamp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timestamp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isFinite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;TOLERANCE_SECONDS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Stripe: HMAC-SHA256 over "{t}.{raw body}", compared with each v1 value.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/webhooks/stripe"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Stripe-Signature"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;secret&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pairs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;","&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"="&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timestamp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pairs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"t"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;signatures&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pairs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"v1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

