    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Stale Slack timestamp"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expected&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"v0="&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createHmac&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sha256"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`v0:${timestamp}:${req.body.toString("utf8")}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;digest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"hex"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;timingSafeMatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;received&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;401&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Invalid Slack signature"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;listen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Webhook server listening on port 3000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In Next.js App Router route handlers, read the body with &lt;code&gt;await request.text()&lt;/code&gt; rather than &lt;code&gt;await request.json()&lt;/code&gt; before verifying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 4: Let the inspector check the signature
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The inspector can also tell you whether a captured request carries a valid signature for your secret. This is available on Starter and higher, on durable tunnels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open the tunnel and find Webhook signatures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Choose the provider (Stripe, GitHub, Slack or Shopify), paste the signing secret and save. The secret must be at least 6 characters, is stored encrypted, and is never shown again after you save it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send a webhook from the provider and open it in the inspector. The request detail shows whether the signature is valid.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To confirm the check works, send a request with a wrong signature and see that the inspector reports it as not valid. Choose Remove under Webhook signatures to delete the secret. This check only reports on captured requests. It does not stop an invalid request from reaching your app, so your handler must still verify signatures itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the inspector says the signature is invalid, compare the secret in your &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; file with the one in the provider dashboard. A common slip is pasting an API key or OAuth client ID where a webhook signing secret belongs. Also check that &lt;code&gt;express.raw()&lt;/code&gt; is registered before any &lt;code&gt;express.json()&lt;/code&gt; that could match the same route.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Replay a captured request after the fix
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After you fix your handler, you do not need to create a new checkout session, push a commit or place a new order. Select the request in the inspector, choose Replay, review the dialog, and confirm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dialog shows the method, path, headers and, if it was kept, the body. You can edit them. Because bodies are only stored when Keep bodies was on at capture time, a request captured without a body replays without one and will fail signature verification. Check the dialog for the signature header and the body before you confirm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the following in mind:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The replay goes to the tunnel’s public address and on to your app, and it carries an &lt;code&gt;X-Instawormhole-Replay&lt;/code&gt; header.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You can replay once every 5 seconds, and each replay is recorded in the workspace audit log.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The replayed request appears as a new row in the inspector.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Replay is not available on a password-protected tunnel and is not offered for WebSocket requests.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If the request creates or changes something, replaying it can do that again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Timestamps during replay
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GitHub and Shopify do not put a timestamp inside the signed material, so a captured delivery still verifies later against the same secret. Stripe and Slack bind a timestamp into the signature. If you replay one after the five-minute window, your freshness check will reject it even though the HMAC itself matches. Either replay within the window or temporarily widen the tolerance in development only, and keep strict enforcement in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Best practices
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Document secret formats:&lt;/strong&gt; Keep a &lt;code&gt;.env.example&lt;/code&gt; showing the expected shape, such as &lt;code&gt;STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...&lt;/code&gt;, so teammates notice when they paste an API key by mistake.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Do not echo digests:&lt;/strong&gt; Return a generic error on signature failure instead of the expected HMAC, even in staging.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test idempotency:&lt;/strong&gt; After a request passes, replay it a few times and confirm your handler deduplicates on a delivery identifier such as the Stripe event ID (&lt;code&gt;evt_...&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;code&gt;X-GitHub-Delivery&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;X-Shopify-Webhook-Id&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Use test modes:&lt;/strong&gt; Point only test or sandbox webhooks at a local tunnel.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://instawormhole.com/blog/verify-webhook-signatures-in-request-inspector" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://instawormhole.com/blog/verify-webhook-signatures-in-request-inspector&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A CMS upload endpoint is a path from an untrusted client to persistent storage. If an attacker can turn an uploaded file into executable server code, that path can become web shell access. The security decision is therefore bigger than whether a request looks suspicious: who decides what may be stored, and what prevents that stored object from running?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For teams deciding how to block malicious file uploads on a CMS, compare two configuration choices: concentrating enforcement at the reverse proxy, or combining proxy filtering with strict CMS acceptance and storage rules. The second gives each layer a decision it can actually support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Choice A: Concentrate upload enforcement at the WAF
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A WAF operates before the CMS handles a request. SafeLine documents an HTTP/HTTPS reverse proxy architecture that receives application traffic, filters attacks, and forwards accepted traffic upstream. Its application setup uses a domain, listening port, and upstream address, followed by DNS pointing to the SafeLine server. &lt;a href="https://docs.waf.chaitin.com/en/GetStarted/AddApplication" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official application configuration documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This placement provides a useful interception point. It also creates a routing requirement: an attacker must not be able to bypass it by reaching an exposed origin address or alternate hostname. Restrict origin access using infrastructure controls appropriate to your deployment, and test every public upload route, including API endpoints used by plugins or mobile clients.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limitation is the decision boundary. An HTTP request passing inspection does not establish that its file is safe to publish, process, or execute. A legitimate-looking image upload might still target a vulnerable image processor. An authenticated request might carry a file type the business never intended to accept.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The supplied SafeLine documentation describes web attack filtering, but does not establish a CMS-specific upload integration, antivirus scanning, archive inspection, or guaranteed detection of malicious uploaded files. Do not make those capabilities assumptions in your design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Choice B: Filter requests, then enforce acceptance inside the CMS
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this configuration, the proxy handles incoming attack traffic while the application decides whether an upload is authorized and acceptable. Storage and server configuration determine what can happen afterward.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider a general web shell attack sequence, rather than a claimed incident. An attacker discovers a plugin upload route, submits server-side code disguised as an allowed file, and then requests its stored URL. If the server executes it, the attacker gains a foothold that may support credential theft or further compromise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The defender should investigate three evidence sources together:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Request evidence: upload route, method, response status, authenticated identity, and repeated filename or content-type variations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Application and storage evidence: whether an object was created, its generated identifier, detected format, storage location, and validation result.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Execution evidence: subsequent requests to the object, unexpected child processes from the web service, or unusual outbound connections.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A blocked upload request is evidence of an attempted action. An object appearing in storage shows that an upload reached a later stage. Unexpected execution changes the investigation into a possible compromise. These observations call for different responses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make the acceptance boundary explicit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with a narrow list of business-required file formats. Validate extensions and actual content with suitable parsers; a client-supplied MIME type is only a claim. Where appropriate, decode and re-encode accepted images before publication. Keep those processing libraries patched and constrain their resource use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate storage names on the server. Never allow a supplied filename to determine a filesystem path. Apply upload authorization, size limits, quotas, and CSRF protection where cookie-based authentication requires it. Treat plugin and API upload routes as part of the same inventory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Store uploads outside executable application directories. Where files must be served publicly, configure the serving layer so uploaded objects cannot invoke server-side interpreters. Separate storage can help, but verify its access policy and serving behavior rather than assuming separation alone is sufficient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If malware scanning is part of your architecture, quarantine files until the required checks finish. A pending scan must not leave a downloadable or processable object available through another route.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SafeLine can supply another source of request evidence: its application guide describes an attack list in the console and a detail view for individual attacks. Correlate that information with CMS and server logs; it cannot independently prove whether a file was stored or executed. &lt;a href="https://docs.waf.chaitin.com/en/GetStarted/AddApplication" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official attack verification documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the boundary before enabling uploads
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a staging environment and harmless fixtures. Confirm that a valid upload succeeds, a disallowed format fails, a MIME mismatch fails, and an oversized request is rejected at the intended layer. Check that rejected files leave no publicly accessible object behind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then verify that uploaded files cannot execute and that direct origin access cannot bypass the proxy. Include legitimate bulk uploads in testing so operational limits do not silently break editorial workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Can SafeLine replace CMS upload validation?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep CMS validation in place. SafeLine's documented role is filtering and monitoring HTTP traffic in front of applications. That role does not establish whether a stored file meets your application's acceptance policy. &lt;a href="https://docs.waf.chaitin.com/en/home" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official SafeLine overview&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Should every upload endpoint use a CAPTCHA?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Evaluate the client first. SafeLine documents CAPTCHA challenges for bot protection, but an interactive challenge can disrupt API clients and automated publishing. It also does not validate the uploaded file's contents. &lt;a href="https://docs.waf.chaitin.com/en/home" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official feature overview&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What if a suspicious file is already present?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Restrict access to it, preserve relevant evidence, and determine whether it executed. Review associated sessions and credentials, and investigate persistence. Deleting the file alone does not resolve a compromised server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Which configuration should a security owner choose?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose proxy filtering plus CMS acceptance rules and non-executable storage. Assign an owner to each boundary and require evidence that it works. A successful test should demonstrate both that unwanted uploads are rejected and that accepted objects cannot become executable application code.&lt;/p&gt;




