&lt;p&gt;This is part 3 of &lt;em&gt;My First Agentic Coding: Field Notes&lt;/em&gt;, a series about the five months I spent growing software through instructions to Claude Code alone, without writing a line of code myself. Each part covers one topic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In short:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;JSVisualizer has 52 ADRs. Claude, not I, decided what became an ADR.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handing that off worked in some respects. But some records exist only because I asked for them, and one ADR Claude wrote didn't match my intent. The mismatch was sitting in a plan I had skimmed and approved.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You can delegate what to record, but only a human can check whether a decision matches what they meant.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An ADR (Architecture Decision Record) is a short document that records one design decision and the reasons behind it. CLAUDE.md describes a project as it is now and is loaded at the start of every session. An ADR, by contrast, records why something was decided at the time, and you consult it when you need it. In my projects, ADRs live in &lt;code&gt;docs/adr/&lt;/code&gt;, one file per decision, like this (headings only, translated from Japanese):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;# ADR-014: Don't fix the recursion depth limit for now
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## Status
## Context
## Decision
## Consequences
## Alternatives
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The tool I'm building, &lt;a href="https://tntetsu.github.io/JSVisualizer/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JSVisualizer&lt;/a&gt;, is a web app that runs a JavaScript program one step at a time and visualizes what happens. (&lt;a href="https://dev.to/tntetsu/see-your-javascript-run-12-different-ways-meet-jsvisualizer-and-how-it-differs-from-python-tutor-1kk5"&gt;A separate series&lt;/a&gt; introduces it.) As of November 4, 2026, it has 52 ADRs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yet I have never had my own criteria for what deserves an ADR. As I described in &lt;a href="https://dev.to/tntetsu/five-months-without-writing-a-line-of-code-the-basic-loop-of-agentic-coding-4akd"&gt;part 1&lt;/a&gt;, at a stopping point I just said "Document this and commit," and Claude decided the rest. On top of that, I've read almost none of the ADRs it wrote.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In part 1, I promised to discuss in part 3 whether this was a good idea. The short answer: half of it worked, and half of it fell short.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quotes from my sessions below are translated from Japanese.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  At first, there were no ADRs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I started JSVisualizer on May 25, and for the first three weeks there were no ADRs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As the code grew, I began to worry that I would forget my own past design decisions. I told Claude I wanted to keep a record of our sessions for future design and maintenance, and Claude suggested ADRs. The format itself was Claude's idea.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On June 14, &lt;a href="https://github.com/tntetsu/JSVisualizer/commit/01fa8e7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ADR-001 through 015 were created all at once&lt;/a&gt;. Claude pieced together three weeks of decisions from the git log and design documents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ADRs are meant to be written when the decision is made, so this isn't a model to follow. I see it as a mistake on my part. But after that one catch-up batch, starting with &lt;a href="https://github.com/tntetsu/JSVisualizer/blob/main/docs/adr/ADR-020-try-catch-host-exception.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ADR-020&lt;/a&gt;, almost every ADR was written on the same day as its decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JSInterpreter, the library JSVisualizer depends on, was even later. Its ADR-001 through 011 were written in August, during my retrospective.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Claude decided what became an ADR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The process was simple. At the end of a session, I'd say "Document this and commit," and Claude would decide that something was worth an ADR and write it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I never once turned down one of Claude's suggestions. A few times, I asked for a particular decision to be documented with extra care. So my involvement came down to two things: asking at stopping points, and emphasizing especially important decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Looking back over the 52 ADRs, a pattern emerges. Conventions the whole codebase follows, merging or removing features, and fixes involving language semantics became ADRs. Small display tweaks were handled by editing CLAUDE.md.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That looks like a sensible line. But it isn't my line. It's a pattern I found afterward by looking at the judgments Claude made session by session.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So far, this is a story of delegation working. I never had to think up ADR criteria, and the record kept growing. The problem was what fell through the cracks when I delegated everything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When I had to ask for the record
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On October 5, I reported three bugs in a row. (The heat map shows how many times each line ran. The execution trace lists executed statements one row at a time.) Claude fixed all three, but only one got an ADR.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Bug&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;ADR&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;console.log(010)&lt;/code&gt; printed &lt;code&gt;10&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Claude wrote one (&lt;a href="https://github.com/tntetsu/JSInterpreter/blob/master/docs/adr/ADR-012-strict-mode-numeric-literals.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JSInterpreter ADR-012&lt;/a&gt;, in Japanese, like the other ADRs linked here)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The heat map showed twice the actual run count&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;None&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The execution trace showed the same statement twice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;None&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;010&lt;/code&gt; fix involved a choice. In JavaScript, it's 8 in sloppy mode and a syntax error in strict mode. Choosing one is a decision, so Claude recorded the reasons and alternatives in an ADR. Claude had first implemented the sloppy-mode rule and changed course after I asked, "By the way, what happens in strict mode?" The ADR also lists that first approach among the alternatives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the other two, Claude didn't write an ADR. They looked like ordinary bug fixes, so I understand why. But I wanted them on record:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I want to keep a record of why this happened and why it's worth fixing, so please add an ADR.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The resulting ADR-042 explains that both bugs had the same cause. Under the definition in &lt;a href="https://github.com/tntetsu/JSVisualizer/blob/main/docs/adr/ADR-008-humanstep-definition.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ADR-008&lt;/a&gt;, a single statement like &lt;code&gt;console.log('A', i);&lt;/code&gt; takes two steps: "start of statement" and "call completed." But both views treated one step as "one execution" or "one row." The definition and its use had drifted apart. ADR-042 also records a decision: keep the definition as it is, and have the views build a "statement execution" unit on their side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I learned: &lt;strong&gt;when a bug comes from misreading a spec, the record of the fix becomes a record of how to read the spec correctly.&lt;/strong&gt; The next time someone builds a view, it can prevent the same misreading. Even fixes that Claude didn't turn into ADRs were worth keeping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  "Make a note of it" became an ADR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's also an example of the opposite: Claude turning a casual request of mine into a proper record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On November 4, I had Claude investigate why JSInterpreter stopped on deep recursion. After reading the measurements, I said:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I've decided not to fix the recursion depth for now. But please make a note in the JSInterpreter project of what we found this time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude turned this into &lt;a href="https://github.com/tntetsu/JSInterpreter/blob/master/docs/adr/ADR-014-recursion-depth-limit.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ADR-014&lt;/a&gt;. It contains the measurements, the cause, the decision not to fix it for now, and options for when it's revisited. Things that weren't measured are marked "not measured."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A decision &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; to fix something leaves no trace in git or in the code. Without the ADR, the next person to hit the problem would have to measure everything again. Leaving it to Claude to decide what form my "note" should take worked well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The ADR recorded the implementation, not my intent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the heart of this post.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On September 24, an "Arrays" column was added to the execution trace, showing a small diagram of each array on every row. The diagram puts labels at the positions of variables used as indexes, such as &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt;. (JSVisualizer calls these "pointers.")&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On November 2, while looking at an assignment for my class, I noticed something wrong with that column. For code where the index is just a number, like &lt;code&gt;table[3] = 25;&lt;/code&gt;, no array was shown at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude traced the cause to ADR-037, written on September 24. It said to "draw only arrays where at least one pointer is detected," and the code did exactly that. Claude changed the code to always draw arrays and wrote ADR-048 as a decision to "change the earlier design policy."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After reading the report, I replied:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the earlier design, what I meant was: "If a variable used as an index is found, treat its value as an array index and draw it." I never intended to show only the arrays where an index variable was found.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I had in mind was "if an index variable is found, put a label at that position." The condition "if none is found, don't draw the array" was never my intent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude replied:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ADR-048 is written as "changing the earlier design policy," so I'll rewrite it as "fixing ADR-037, which was implemented and recorded differently from the intent."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/tntetsu/JSVisualizer/blob/main/docs/adr/ADR-048-exectrace-array-always.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ADR-048&lt;/a&gt; was rewritten, and ADR-037 got a note in its status and in the relevant passage saying "fixed in ADR-048." The body of ADR-037 was left as written, as a record of that time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I had let it pass, the ADR history would have kept telling &lt;strong&gt;a story that never happened&lt;/strong&gt;: "At first the design filtered arrays, and later it changed." A change of policy and a correction to the record mean very different things to someone reading them later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The condition was in a plan I approved
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That isn't the end of the story. When I reread the session logs for this post, I found the condition in Claude's plan from September 24:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Scan the arrays in &lt;code&gt;arrayVarNames&lt;/code&gt; from that step's &lt;code&gt;envMap&lt;/code&gt;, and use &lt;code&gt;detectPointerVars(envMap, arrName, arr, subscriptVars, arrayVarNames)&lt;/code&gt; to render &lt;strong&gt;only the arrays where at least one pointer was detected at that step&lt;/strong&gt;,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I did read this plan before approving it, but I only skimmed it. It looked fine, so I approved it. Looking back, I think this happened because I didn't read it carefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In part 1, I wrote that you can reject an approach at the plan stage. This is the flip side. The plan was about 4,700 characters long, and this line was surrounded by function names. Reading it now, I can see it means "programs whose indexes are plain numbers won't show any arrays." But it doesn't say that in so many words.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why it happened
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If Claude writes the ADRs, they record what Claude sees: the code, its understanding of my instructions, and the approved plan. ADR-037 followed the approved plan, so from Claude's point of view, nothing was wrong. The intent in my head doesn't get into an ADR unless I put it into words and check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An ADR is supposed to record &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; a decision was made. When you delegate it to an AI, the "why" can end up holding a description of the implementation instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ADR-037's status was "Accepted." But that word was Claude's. I never read the ADR, let alone accepted it. In fact, I've read almost none of the ADRs. I started keeping them so the record would be there when I needed it, and I didn't read them as they were written. As I said in part 1, I don't read the code either. If you only skim the plan and read neither the code nor the ADRs, a mismatch with your intent stays until you notice it in use. This time, that took 39 days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Still, keeping the record paid off. Because ADR-037 existed, I could see what had supposedly been decided, and I could say "That's not what I meant." It did serve the purpose I started it for: being there when needed. What was missing was a check at the moment of deciding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I would do now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd split what to delegate from what to keep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Delegate&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Keep for yourself&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The first call on whether something becomes an ADR&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Asking at stopping points, and flagging important decisions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The format and wording of ADRs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Asking for the "why" behind bug fixes to be recorded&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Organizing things like measurements&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Checking that plans and decisions match your intent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The last check is the one I most want to change. Reading every plan and every ADR carefully is a lot of work. So I'd ask Claude to report each decision and its reason in two or three lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You might think this is unnecessary. What was decided and why should already have been agreed on in the conversation before the ADR is written. Even so, I think a report and a check would catch mismatches with my intent much earlier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The key is to have Claude report &lt;strong&gt;what the user will experience&lt;/strong&gt;, not implementation details. If ADR-037's condition had been described as "Programs whose indexes are plain numbers won't show any arrays," I think I would have said "No" on the spot. Before approving a plan, you could ask something like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Before I approve this, please explain, without implementation terms, what won't be displayed under this plan and for which kinds of programs.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Note that "Document this and commit" only started producing ADRs after ADRs were already in place. If you're starting out, I'd first ask, "Please record decisions and their reasons as ADRs in &lt;code&gt;docs/adr/&lt;/code&gt;," and add rules to CLAUDE.md, for example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## ADRs
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;When a change involves an important design decision, add an ADR in the same commit.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;After writing an ADR, report the decision, the reason, and its effect on users in two or three lines.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;When a bug is caused by what an existing ADR says, check with the human whether that ADR matched their intent.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;Distinguish "a change of policy" from "a correction to the record." Don't rewrite the original ADR's body; add a note instead.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These instructions and rules are examples I came up with while looking back, not things I have actually done. The following instruction for recording a bug fix is one I actually sent (translated here):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;I want to keep a record of why this happened and why it's worth fixing, so please add an ADR.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Never turning down a suggestion doesn't prove the suggestions were good.&lt;/strong&gt; I may simply have had no criteria for turning them down.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;I can't tell whether the rule helped.&lt;/strong&gt; In August, I added a rule to CLAUDE.md about writing ADRs as decisions are made, and ADRs became more frequent afterward. But several other factors changed at the same time, so I can't credit the rule. And writing ADRs on the day of the decision had already started in June, before the rule.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;I don't know how many other mismatches like ADR-048 exist.&lt;/strong&gt; I haven't reread all 52 ADRs to check.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Some of this relies on my memory:&lt;/strong&gt; that Claude suggested ADRs, and that I skimmed the plan. I haven't verified in the logs the moment Claude suggested ADRs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;I haven't tried the reporting approach yet.&lt;/strong&gt; I don't know whether it works.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;This is one developer's experience with a limited set of projects.&lt;/strong&gt; On a team, other people would read the ADRs, and things would probably be different.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] At stopping points, explicitly ask the AI to document the work and commit. It's fine to let the AI make the first call on what becomes an ADR.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Have ADRs written when decisions are made. Treat writing them all later as a way to catch up, not as standard practice.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] For especially important decisions, ask for extra care.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] When a bug comes from misreading a spec, ask for the "why" to be recorded.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Record decisions not to fix something, too.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Before approving a plan, have its conditions and limits explained in terms of what users will see.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] When an ADR is written, have the decision, the reason, and the effect on users reported in two or three lines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] When a bug is caused by what an ADR says, check whether that ADR matched your intent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Make sure "a change of policy" and "a correction to the record" are written up differently. Don't rewrite the original ADR; add a note.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Next time
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next time: "The AI reads old documents and writes new mistakes." ADRs may keep getting written, but the documents that pull them together fall behind. And outdated text left in CLAUDE.md gets carried into the new text the AI writes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The models used in the exchanges in this post were Claude Sonnet 4.6 in June, Claude Sonnet 5 in September, and Claude Opus 5.5 in October and November.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1b6rgzgf2xro7bhnb95r.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1b6rgzgf2xro7bhnb95r.png" alt=" " width="800" height="533"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So you are about to put an AI classifier in front of a few hundred thousand files. The vendor says it sorts automatically and the demo looks effortless. Before you approve it, there is a set of questions worth asking — not about features, but about how you would know, six months in, whether it actually works. This guide is about the evaluation side of that decision. It is about method, not any particular product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why "it classifies automatically" is not a claim
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The phrase describes a capability, not a result. It tells you nothing about how the classification behaves on your data, which is the only data that matters. To turn the phrase into something testable, you need three things: a reference to compare against, a way to probe the edges, and a plan for when it is wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Almost every team skips straight to deployment and discovers these gaps later, usually at the moment someone asks for a document and cannot find it. That is an expensive place to learn. The good news is that the evaluation itself is not complicated. It is mostly a matter of being deliberate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step one: build a labelled set
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before the tool runs, take a few hundred representative files and assign categories by hand. This is your evaluation set, and its quality determines everything that follows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Representative is the operative word. If your real corpus is full of scanned invoices and chat screenshots, a labelled set made entirely of cleanly typed reports will tell you nothing useful. Include the awkward material on purpose. Include the thing that got misfiled last quarter. Include the document with an embedded table and three images, because that is exactly the case where classifiers tend to either handle the whole file gracefully or tear it into fragments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The point of the labelled set is not to prove the tool is good. It is to create a fixed reference so that "seems accurate" becomes a number you can repeat to a colleague, and so that a future change can be measured against the same baseline. Without it you are stuck with impressions, and impressions drift.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step two: measure more than accuracy
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run the classifier on the labelled set and compare. Resist the urge to stop at a single accuracy percentage. Aggregate accuracy hides the distribution of errors, and the distribution is where your risk lives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Look at three separate things.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first is per-category error. Do mistakes concentrate in one or two categories? A system that is 95% accurate overall but fails on every scanned contract is not 95% useful to a legal team. Break the number down by category and by file type.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second is consistency. Feed the same document under a different name. Rephrase a title the way a human would ("quotation" becomes "pricing reply"). If a small cosmetic change flips the category, the classifier is keyed on surface signals rather than content, and it will be brittle in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The third is the failure mode. When the tool is uncertain, what does it do? A system that always emits a category, even when the material is thin, is more dangerous than one that marks the item as uncertain and asks for review. Silent confidence is the failure mode to watch for, because it looks identical to success in a summary report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step three: probe the edges on purpose
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a practice in ML evaluation called adversarial testing — deliberately constructing inputs designed to break the system. You do not need a research team to do a version of this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take a contract with a table and images embedded in it. Does the system treat it as one document or split it apart? Take a file whose name contradicts its content. Does it trust the name or read the file? Take a document written in a language your team rarely uses. Does it degrade gracefully? These are not exotic cases; in a real shared drive they are Tuesday.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reason to do this deliberately is that edge cases define the operational envelope. Once you know the system handles clean digital documents well but stumbles on scans, you can design the workflow around that: trust it on the first, put a review step on the second. That is a far more useful outcome than a binary verdict of "works" or "doesn't work".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A note on where the signal comes from
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is worth understanding what the classifier actually has to work with, because it changes what you should expect. Classification is more reliable when the system has richer information about each file — not just its name and type, but the properties and structure it can inspect. Some tools expand the set of file attributes they read as part of their development; the practical effect is more evidence for each decision, which shows up as steadier categories on messy inputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This matters for your evaluation too. If you are comparing two systems, a difference in results may come down to how much information each one has available, not which one has a better decision process. That is a fair thing to test, but it is worth naming so you know what you are measuring.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What changed when the input got richer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One observation from watching these systems evolve is worth folding into your evaluation. Classification quality is bounded by the information the system can inspect. When a tool expands the set of properties it reads from each file — not just the name and type, but structural and descriptive attributes — each decision gets more evidence to work with. The practical signature of this is steadier categories on inputs that used to confuse it: files with messy names, files whose content spans several topics, files where the obvious label is misleading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For your evaluation, this means a version-to-version improvement might show up less as a higher headline number and more as fewer failures in the categories that were previously weakest. If you keep the same labelled set across evaluations, you will see it as a reshaping of the error distribution rather than a uniform lift. That is worth knowing, because it tells you where to invest your own effort: enriching the metadata you feed in often pays off more than tweaking thresholds downstream.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also means you should compare systems on equivalent footing. A difference in results may come down to how much each system can see, not which has the better decision logic. Ask what signals each one uses before attributing a gap to the algorithm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step four: decide what "wrong" costs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part engineers often hand to someone else, and it should not be handed off. A wrong classification has a cost, and that cost is not uniform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A marketing deck filed under the wrong project is an annoyance. A signed contract filed as a generic document, where nobody notices and nobody can restore it, is a real problem. The evaluation question is not just "how often is it wrong" but "when it is wrong, can we see it and can we undo it".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That question points at a product capability worth checking explicitly: are classification results editable? Can a person correct a tag, and does the system learn from the correction? A system that proposes and lets a human dispose is operationally safer than one that acts unilaterally, precisely because it assumes its own fallibility. In evaluation terms, the existence of a correction path is part of the system, not a workaround around it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step five: know the retrieval dependency
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more thing tends to get conflated. Automatic classification and semantic search are related but distinct, and the order matters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Search that works on content rather than filenames depends on a foundation of organised material. When files carry categories and tags, the search space narrows before matching begins, which improves both the hit rate and the response time. Without that foundation, a semantic search box is doing expensive work over an undifferentiated pile. If your evaluation shows good classification, you should expect search to improve; if classification is weak, no amount of search sophistication will compensate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also why archiving rules are relevant to an ostensibly technical decision. Regulations in many places now treat electronic records as equivalent to paper ones and expect them to be reproducible years later in readable form. A system that classifies, tags and preserves provenance is not just a convenience — it is what makes the retrieval requirement achievable. The policy sets the requirement; the engineering has to meet it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A framework table for the decision
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the comparison in the form of a general selection framework. It describes approaches, not products.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Dimension&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Manual filing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Filename and folder rules&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;AI classification&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up-front effort&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low, continuous human time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Medium, needs a naming scheme&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Medium, configured once&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Consistency&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Varies by person&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High, reads names only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High, reads content&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;After rename or move&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Easy to lose track&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rules break, redo needed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Recognised from content&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retrieval&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Browse from memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Filename keywords&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Semantic, plain-language&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data leaves device&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Depends on tool&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Can run locally&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Best fit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hundreds of files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stable, structured sets&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Large, mixed, legacy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Main risk&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Breaks under load&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fails on messy names&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Needs initial spot-checking&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Note: relative comparisons within the same scenario, not absolute scores. Results depend on file types and team habits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Closing thought
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The instinct when evaluating an AI system is to look for the number that settles the question. There is rarely such a number. What you can get is a labelled set, a breakdown of errors, a map of the edges, and a clear answer to what happens when it is wrong. That is enough to make the decision with your eyes open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tooling in this space is mature enough to be worth evaluating seriously, and varied enough that a quick search will surface several options. The method you bring to the evaluation is the part that is genuinely yours. Build the labelled set. Probe the edges. Decide what a mistake costs. The rest tends to follow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Plango (installplango.com), from the official docs and launch materials. Independent notes; not affiliated with Plango's makers. Links to my free cheat sheet and starter pack are at the end.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your coding agent writes the app in minutes. Then it keeps working — while you sleep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the deal we all made with Claude Code, Cursor, and Codex: the agent reads your files, runs commands, and talks to the internet on your behalf, fast and unsupervised. The part nobody put in the brochure is what else it can reach while it works: your SSH keys, your &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; files, your credentials, a plugin with hidden instructions, a server address you have never heard of.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plango is the security app built for exactly that problem. Here's how it works, based on the official site, its pricing page, and the makers' launch materials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Two risks, three axes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The makers frame it as two risks: external AI attackers finding exploits, and your own agent taking unsafe actions. Plango's local AI security model evaluates threats around three things they call the "lethal trifecta" of agent security: &lt;strong&gt;data access, communication, and exposure&lt;/strong&gt;. What can the agent reach, where can it send things, and what is it exposing to the world.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the part that matters most for a security tool: it runs entirely on your device. Your data stays on-device, private by default. A guard that uploads your &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; files to someone else's server would be a worse problem than the one it solves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The setup that matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The highest-value configuration takes about ten minutes, and it is almost embarrassingly simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Restrict the obvious.&lt;/strong&gt; SSH keys and &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; files are the two things an agent should almost never need to read. In Plango, restrict agent access to them. If an agent genuinely needs a specific key for one specific task, grant it for that task only — the makers describe exactly this use case — and take the access away when the task is done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tag what matters.&lt;/strong&gt; Pick a Finder color tag and give it a meaning: red means agents never enter. Tag the folders holding client work, financial records, and credentials, then tell Plango red-tagged folders are out of bounds. A new folder is protected the moment you tag it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Test the boundary.&lt;/strong&gt; Ask your agent to read a protected file and watch what happens. You want to see a block in the dashboard. A guard you have never seen block anything is a guard you have not tested.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then move your secrets. Plango scans projects for plain-text keys in &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; files and config files and offers to move them into the macOS Keychain, where the agent cannot read the raw values. One caution the makers' own materials support: moving a secret does not revoke it. If a key was ever committed to git or pasted into a chat, rotate it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The network view, and the grade that made me pause
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The network view shows every connection — which app or agent made it, and where it went. Watch it for a few days before blocking anything; you are building an allow list of the destinations your work actually needs, and the blocks come after.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feature I underestimated is the skill and plugin scan. Agent skills, plugins, and MCP servers are code you invite into your workflow, and either one can contain hidden instructions — lines that tell the agent to do something the description never mentions. Plango reads the source and grades it A+ to F, with each finding tied to a file and line, before you install it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The makers publish these grades publicly, and browsing them is sobering: an official Stripe plugin is graded D, because several of its tools pre-approve command-line actions and remote installer scripts to run without asking — so a tricked agent could silently move money. A grade is an automated review of the source at one revision. It is evidence, not a verdict — but it is a reason to look closer before you install.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The overnight checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The makers' tagline is "lets your AI agents work all night — safely." Read that as a design goal, not a guarantee: the safety comes from the rules you wrote before you went to sleep. Before any unattended session, I run seven checks: the agent is one Plango guards; the task's files are outside red-tagged folders; no SSH key or &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; is readable; secrets are in the Keychain; the network allow list covers the task; every skill or plugin in use has a grade I have read; and I know what "done" looks like — and what the agent should do if it gets stuck (stop and wait, not improvise). If any check fails, the session waits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the morning, the dashboard is the first thing I read: what the agent did, what Plango blocked, what it requested, and anything anomalous — a new destination, a file outside the task's scope, activity at an odd hour.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Honest limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's brand new.&lt;/strong&gt; First Product Hunt launch October 22, 2026; re-launch November 23, 2026. Expect fast movement; re-check the official site.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mac and Windows only.&lt;/strong&gt; Mac needs Apple silicon and macOS 15+; Windows needs Windows 11. No Linux version is advertised.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Plango cannot act by itself.&lt;/strong&gt; It guards, logs, and blocks. It does not fix your code or rotate your keys. The fixing is yours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No tool makes an unsupervised agent safe in general.&lt;/strong&gt; Plango reduces specific risks by specific mechanisms. Your judgment is still the last line.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It costs money, eventually, if you want the extras.&lt;/strong&gt; From the official pricing page (checked November 24, 2026): Free is $0 — the guard, the scanners, the firewall, one computer. Paid is $4.99/month and adds Plango Chat, a more advanced guard model, and any number of computers. New accounts get Paid free for 7 days with no card, and the trial never turns into a paid plan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more thing, because the name will cost someone money: the security tool is at &lt;strong&gt;installplango.com&lt;/strong&gt;. PlanGo (plango.pro) is construction scheduling software. PlaninGo plans trips. Planango manages tasks. Check the domain before you pay anyone.&lt;/p&gt;




